---
/*
  /afford-lab — a LOCAL-ONLY bench, dev-gated below, linked from nowhere.

  ▶ DECIDED AND SHIPPED, 2026-08-07: **E, on both surfaces**, plus `glyphs:
  off`, `cta: below`, `beckon: focus`, and the bloom moved onto focus. Michael,
  after driving it: "this is good. I say let's put this into production."
  It lives in app.css ("The overview's stars" / "The Sky"), index.astro,
  ConstellationSuite.astro and scripts/focus-mode.ts. NOT shipped: the intro
  line and `press` (both still `off` here), and `cta: inline`.

  This page is kept rather than deleted — the same call /sky-lab and
  /reveal-lab got — because A–D are the only record of what E is made of, and
  the argument for spending three signals on one job is illegible without them.
  Delete it when the question stops being live.

  ⚠ It has since DIVERGED from production on purpose: this page still owns the
  `input:` switch (production reads the device and offers no override) and
  still renders treatments A–D. Do not read it as the current design — read
  app.css. What is authoritative here is the REASONING, and the ability to put
  the rejected options back on screen next to the shipped one.

  THE QUESTION. Michael, 2026-08-06, after browsing the live site as a stranger
  on a phone: "it's hard to tell that I should click the constellations, or
  that I can click certain reflections when inside the constellations."

  THE CAUSE, stated narrowly so this page can test it: **every affordance in
  the Sky is a `:hover` rule.** On the overview the row is a link, but at rest
  the name is plain `base-content` — no underline, no colour, no mark — and all
  of the "this is a door" signal lives in app.css `.group:hover .sky-name`.
  Inside a suite it is worse, because the list is MIXED: only writing stanzas
  open anything, and the distinction between a tappable essay and an inert
  quote is carried entirely by `.suite-writing:hover`.

  ⚠ AND THE LAMPLIGHT IMPERSONATES IT. `.is-lit` warms a quote's blockquote
  and a writing's title with the same gesture. On touch that is the ONLY
  feedback the page ever gives, so the one dynamic thing a phone sees actively
  teaches "everything here glows" — which is the opposite of "this one opens."
  Scroll §2 in `input: touch` and watch a quote light up exactly like an essay.
  That is the bug, and no amount of hover polish reaches it.

  ⚠ THE MOST IMPORTANT CONTROL IS `input:`. Resizing a desktop browser to 390px
  does NOT reproduce a phone — the pointer is still a mouse and every hover rule
  still fires, so the page keeps handing you an affordance a thumb will never
  receive. `input: touch` switches the whole model at once. Judge there first;
  pointer is the flattering view, and it is the one that misled us.

  ⚠ AND `input:` IS NOT A HOVER SWITCH — IT PICKS ONE OF TWO MODELS. This began
  as `hover: on/off`, which only ever subtracted, so proximity kept running
  underneath and a desktop had TWO systems narrating at once: rows lighting as
  you scrolled while the cursor lit a different one. Michael, 2026-08-07: "it
  gets a little janky or a little too visually busy … now the mouse hover and
  the scrolling are competing for attention."

  The fix is not precedence — precedence only stops them CONTRADICTING each
  other, it cannot stop them both being in motion. Proximity is not a fallback
  hover overrides; it is the answer to a different question ("what am I looking
  at, on a device that cannot point?") and it should not be asked at all where a
  cursor exists. So each mode consults exactly one ambient input, and the other
  is not merely outranked but never read. See `focusTracker` for the table.

  WHERE IT STANDS. Michael drove A–D on 2026-08-07 and called the combination:
  "a combination of B, C, and D is actually the best implementation… they all
  have elements that help guide the user." That is treatment **E**, which the
  page now opens on. A–D are kept because E is only legible next to its parts —
  and because the argument for spending three signals on one job is only
  honest if you can still see what one signal alone bought.

  WHAT TO JUDGE, in this order:
    1. `input: touch`, scroll §1. E should read as ONE thing arriving, not three
       — colour, mark and word are deliberately on the same focus and the same
       easing. If it reads as a checklist of effects, it is over-built.
    1b. Then `input: pointer` and DON'T scroll — move only the cursor. The page
       should now be perfectly still until you point at something. If anything
       is moving that you did not move, the split has leaked.
    2. `beckon:` — focus / always / off. The open question is whether motion at
       REST (always) is invitation or nag on a page of eleven rows. It costs
       nothing on the first visit and everything on the fiftieth.
    3. `cta:` — below / inline / off. Below reserves its own line, so the word
       simply sits there faded and warms with the mark; nothing on the page ever
       moves. Inline holds no width at rest — it is in the name's flex row, so
       any width it took would be width stolen from the name — and therefore
       still has to unfurl the word from zero. Judge inline on a phone, on the
       longest name in the sky: whether that unfurl bumps the name onto an extra
       line is the entire question, and the only reason `below` exists.
    4. `input: touch`, read §2 straight down on suite E. Can you tell, WITHOUT
       tapping, which stanzas open? The claim to test: the lamplight touching
       every stanza stops being a lie once only the openable ones have a word
       for it to warm. If the quotes still read as clickable, it fails.
       (The lamplight ITSELF is not input-conditional and stays on desktop —
       it is atmosphere, and it shipped long before this question. Only the
       affordance riding it went touch-only.)
    5. `glyphs: on` for a moment. Two things to look for — whether the margin
       reads busier or merely different, and what happens to the STARS, which
       were sized to sit behind a glyph that is no longer there.
    6. On a real phone, at the real URL. `press: on` is the tap confirmation
       that is missing everywhere today — it only means anything under a thumb.
    7. Both themes. "Quiet" is a contrast judgement, and E's resting hue in
       particular reads very differently on paper than on dusk.

  WHAT IS NOT IN QUESTION here, so don't re-litigate it:
    · The typeset suite. /sky-lab settled that on 2026-07-23 — each medium in
      its own register, not carded. Carding the writing stanzas would "solve"
      discoverability by discarding the thing worth walking through.
    · WHICH stanzas open. Writing does, quotes and songs don't. That's the
      data model, and it is what makes the suite a harder problem than the
      blog feed — where every card is a trigger, so one learned tap
      generalises and the fade only has to say WHERE.

  ⚠ Real components where it counts. §2 renders the shipped `SuiteStanza` and
  mounts the real `Reader`, so the registers are the genuine article and a tap
  actually opens the essay — you cannot judge whether a cue paid off if the
  thing it promises never arrives. It is deliberately NOT wrapped in
  `.suite-item`/`.suite-writing`: those class names carry app.css's ungated
  hover rules, and the whole point of this page is to gate them. The overview
  in §1 is rebuilt with `.al-` classes for the same reason (which costs the
  StarBloom — it blooms on `.group:hover`, i.e. it is hover-only, i.e. it is
  exactly the kind of affordance under examination).

  ⚠ The suite's arcs and stars ARE drawn here, at some duplication cost,
  because the `mark` treatment puts a ring on the margin glyph and the glyph
  sits on top of a star — if those collide, only a page with both will show it.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Label from '../components/Label.astro';
import Reader from '../components/Reader.astro';
import PostArticle from '../components/PostArticle.astro';
import SuiteStanza from '../components/SuiteStanza.astro';
import StarBloom from '../components/StarBloom.astro';
import { listConstellations, getConstellation, type Constellation, type SuiteItem } from '../lib/constellations';
import type { WritingItem } from '../lib/blog';
import { TYPE_META } from '../lib/fragments-display';

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

const supabase = Astro.locals.supabase;
const refs = await listConstellations(supabase);

// The bench suite has to be a MIXED one or the question evaporates: the problem
// is telling an essay from a quote at a glance, so a constellation of nothing
// but essays would pass a test it was never given. Take the heaviest few and
// prefer one that actually interleaves kinds.
const heaviest = [...refs].sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count).slice(0, 4);
const loaded = (await Promise.all(heaviest.map((c) => getConstellation(supabase, c.slug)))).filter(
  (c): c is Constellation => !!c,
);
const kinds = (c: Constellation) => new Set(c.items.map((i) => i.kind)).size;
const suite = [...loaded].sort((a, b) => kinds(b) - kinds(a) || b.items.length - a.items.length)[0] ?? null;

const writings = (suite?.items ?? [])
  .filter((i): i is Extract<SuiteItem, { kind: 'writing' }> => i.kind === 'writing')
  .map((i) => i.item as WritingItem);

// §3 compares treatments on ONE stanza, so it needs a real writing and the
// quote that would sit next to it — the comparison is only meaningful in pairs.
const sampleWriting = suite?.items.find((i) => i.kind === 'writing') ?? null;
const sampleQuote = suite?.items.find((i) => i.kind === 'quote') ?? null;

const glyph = Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(TYPE_META).map(([k, v]) => [k, v.glyph])) as Record<string, string>;

// ── the overview's type scale, copied from index.astro so the rows are the
// real sizes. Weight is carried mostly by BRIGHTNESS, not size.
const TIERS = [
  'text-4xl sm:text-5xl',
  'text-3xl sm:text-4xl',
  'text-2xl sm:text-3xl',
  'text-xl sm:text-2xl',
  'text-lg sm:text-xl',
];
const STAR_TIERS = ['text-base', 'text-sm', 'text-sm', 'text-xs', 'text-xs'];
const GLOW_TIERS = [1, 0.82, 0.66, 0.5, 0.36];
const distinct = [...new Set(refs.map((r) => r.count))].sort((a, b) => b - a);
const tierIndex = (count: number) => Math.min(distinct.indexOf(count), TIERS.length - 1);
const INDENTS = ['', 'sm:pl-16', 'sm:pl-32'];
const indentOf = (i: number) => INDENTS[i % 3 === 2 ? 2 : i % 2 === 1 ? 1 : 0];

const SUITE_INDENTS = ['0%', '9%', '3%', '15%', '0%', '11%', '5%', '13%'];

// The orientation line counts the sky it is actually standing in — a hardcoded
// "Eleven" would be a lie the first time a constellation is composed, and the
// candidate on trial here is the SENTENCE, so it has to survive being true.
const WORDS = ['No', 'One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four', 'Five', 'Six', 'Seven', 'Eight', 'Nine', 'Ten', 'Eleven', 'Twelve'];
const skyCount = WORDS[refs.length] ?? String(refs.length);

const SKY_PICKS = [
  { key: 'none', label: 'A · none', blurb: 'As shipped. Every signal is a hover rule; on touch the page is a poem.' },
  {
    key: 'lamp',
    label: 'B · lamplight',
    blurb:
      'The row nearest the reading line takes its own colour — proximity, not pointer. The suite’s own gesture, ported up to the one surface where nothing is inert, so it can’t mislead.',
  },
  {
    key: 'rest',
    label: 'C · warm at rest',
    blurb: 'The name permanently carries a trace of its constellation’s colour, so it is not the same ink as prose.',
  },
  {
    key: 'mark',
    label: 'D · a mark',
    blurb: 'A hairline → after the name, at rest. Says “goes somewhere” without saying it in words.',
  },
  {
    key: 'all',
    label: 'E · the composite',
    blurb:
      'B+C+D as one thing: name, mark and word all sit faded at rest and warm together to full strength on the focused row, where the arrow also glows and beckons. Two intensities of one colour, not three signals. The default — Michael, after driving A–D.',
  },
];

const SUITE_PICKS = [
  { key: 'none', label: 'A · none', blurb: 'As shipped. The clamped, faded excerpt is the entire invitation.' },
  {
    key: 'word',
    label: 'B · a word',
    blurb:
      'read on at the tail, set in the small-caps register the attributions already use. Typography, not a button — which was the actual objection.',
  },
  {
    key: 'mark',
    label: 'C · the margin mark',
    blurb:
      '⚠ RETIRED by `glyphs: off` — it rang a glyph that no longer exists. Kept so the comparison still has its record; turn glyphs back on to see it.',
  },
  {
    key: 'rest',
    label: 'D · warm at rest',
    blurb:
      'The title carries a permanent trace of the lamp, so an essay is a different ink from a quote before you touch it.',
  },
  {
    key: 'all',
    label: 'E · the composite',
    blurb:
      'The sky’s philosophy, brought inside: title warm at rest and full on focus, with a faded “Read →” that warms and beckons with it. The lamplight still touches every stanza — but only the ones that OPEN have a word for it to warm, which is what finally makes the passing light mean something instead of impersonating an affordance.',
  },
];
---

<Base title="Affordance lab — It Only Happens Once">
  {/* ── lab bar ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <div class="border-base-300 bg-base-100/85 sticky top-0 z-50 border-b backdrop-blur">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-4xl flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 px-5 py-3">
      <Label variant="chrome" as="span">Afford lab · local only</Label>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1.5 font-sans text-sm">
        {/* The headline control — see the file header for why resizing isn't enough. */}
        <button type="button" id="t-input" class="btn btn-sm normal-case">input: auto</button>
        <span class="text-base-content/25">|</span>
        <span class="text-base-content/40 text-xs">sky</span>
        {
          SKY_PICKS.map((p) => (
            <button type="button" class="lab-pick btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case" data-sky-pick={p.key}>
              {p.label.slice(0, 1)}
            </button>
          ))
        }
        <span class="text-base-content/25">|</span>
        <span class="text-base-content/40 text-xs">suite</span>
        {
          SUITE_PICKS.map((p) => (
            <button type="button" class="lab-pick btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case" data-suite-pick={p.key}>
              {p.label.slice(0, 1)}
            </button>
          ))
        }
        <span class="text-base-content/25">|</span>
        {/* E's own sub-questions — inert under every other sky treatment. */}
        <button type="button" id="t-beckon" class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case">beckon: focus</button>
        <button type="button" id="t-cta" class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case">cta: below</button>
        <button type="button" id="t-glyphs" class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case">glyphs: off</button>
        <span class="text-base-content/25">|</span>
        <button type="button" id="t-intro" class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case">intro: off</button>
        <button type="button" id="t-press" class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case">press: off</button>
        <button type="button" id="t-theme" class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs normal-case">theme</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    /* ⚠ `data-suite` is NOT here. §3's cards each carry their own, and a
      `[data-suite='rest'] .al-writing` written on an ancestor of them would
      match straight through and drag all four to whatever the bar last said —
      which is the one thing a fixed side-by-side may not do. It lives on §2's
      own container instead, so the two scopes can never nest. */
  }
  <main
    class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 py-14 sm:px-6"
    data-sky="all"
    data-beckon="focus"
    data-cta="below"
    data-glyphs="off"
    data-intro="off"
    data-press="off"
  >
    <header class="mb-20">
      <Label variant="voice">prototype · the affordance</Label>
      <h1 class="font-display mt-4 text-4xl leading-[1.1] font-light sm:text-5xl">How do you know it’s a door?</h1>
      <p class="prose-measure text-base-content/70 mt-6 text-lg">
        Every affordance in the Sky is a <code>:hover</code> rule. A thumb receives none of them. This page is four answers
        for the overview and four for the suite, on real constellations, with a switch that takes hover away so you can see
        what a phone actually gets.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-measure text-base-content/50 mt-4 text-sm">
        ⚠ Press <strong>hover: off</strong> before judging anything. Narrowing a desktop window to phone width does not reproduce
        a phone — the pointer is still a mouse, every hover rule still fires, and the page keeps flattering itself with an
        affordance a thumb will never get.
      </p>
      {
        !suite && (
          <p class="text-warning mt-6 font-sans text-sm">
            No constellation with published fragments came back — §2 and §3 are empty. Check the dev database.
          </p>
        )
      }
    </header>

    {/* ═══ 1 · the sky ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    <section class="mb-28">
      <Label as="h2" variant="voice" class="mb-2">1 · The sky — are these names doors?</Label>
      <p class="prose-measure text-base-content/60 mb-8 text-sm">
        The real overview at its real sizes. Every row here is a link; nothing at rest says so. The kindest thing about
        this surface is that <strong>everything on it is tappable</strong> — so unlike the suite, a cue here only has to say
        WHERE, never WHETHER. That is why the lamplight is safe on this page and dangerous on the next one.
      </p>

      <div class="rounded-box border-base-300 bg-base-100 border px-5 py-12 sm:px-12 sm:py-16">
        {
          /* The orientation line — one key for the whole page, rather than chrome
            on every row. Also the visible heading this room has never had: the
            real `/` carries an sr-only h1 and nothing a sighted stranger sees. */
        }
        <p class="al-intro text-base-content/45 mb-12 font-serif text-[1.05rem] italic">
          {skyCount} ways of seeing. Tap one to walk inside.
        </p>

        <div id="al-sky" class="flex flex-col gap-12 sm:gap-16">
          {
            refs.map((c, i) => (
              <div class={indentOf(i)}>
                <a href={`/${c.slug}`} class:list={['al-row block', `cn-${c.color}`]}>
                  <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
                    <span
                      class:list={['al-star', STAR_TIERS[tierIndex(c.count)]]}
                      style={`--glow: ${GLOW_TIERS[tierIndex(c.count)]}`}
                    >
                      {/* The real StarBloom, not a bare mark. It is inert here
                          by construction — app.css triggers it on `.group:hover`
                          and these rows carry no `group` — which is exactly what
                          lets the lab drive it from `.is-focus` instead. */}
                      <StarBloom />
                    </span>
                    <span class={`${TIERS[tierIndex(c.count)]} al-name font-display font-light`}>{c.name}</span>
                    {/* inline placement — see the `cta:` note in the <style> */}
                    <span class="al-cta al-cta--inline" aria-hidden="true">
                      <span class="al-cta__word">Explore</span>
                      <span class="al-cta__arrow">→</span>
                    </span>
                  </div>
                  {c.description && (
                    <p class="text-base-content/55 mt-3 ml-6 max-w-xl font-serif text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
                      {c.description}
                    </p>
                  )}
                  {/* Rendered TWICE, one per placement, because `below` and
                      `inline` are different parents and CSS cannot move a node.
                      Both are aria-hidden: the row is already a link with an
                      accessible name, and appending “Explore →” to all eleven
                      would make a screen reader read the word eleven times to
                      say something the word “link” already said. */}
                  <span class="al-cta al-cta--below" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="al-cta__word">Explore</span>
                    <span class="al-cta__arrow">→</span>
                  </span>
                </a>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          SKY_PICKS.map((p) => (
            <p class="text-base-content/50 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed">
              <strong class="text-base-content/70">{p.label}</strong> — {p.blurb}
            </p>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ═══ 2 · the suite ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    {
      suite && (
        <section class="mb-28">
          <Label as="h2" variant="voice" class="mb-2">
            2 · The suite — which of these open?
          </Label>
          <p class="prose-measure text-base-content/60 mb-8 text-sm">
            “{suite.name}”, real and composed, rendered through the shipped <code>SuiteStanza</code> with the real
            Reader behind it — tap a title and the essay genuinely opens. Read it straight down with hover off and ask,
            before tapping anything, which stanzas do something. Watch the lamplight as you scroll: it warms the quotes
            exactly as brightly as the essays.
          </p>

          <div
            id="al-suite-scope"
            data-suite="none"
            class:list={[
              'rounded-box border-base-300 bg-base-100 border px-5 py-12 sm:px-12 sm:py-16',
              `cn-${suite.color}`,
            ]}
          >
            <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
              <span class="al-star" style="--glow: 1">
                <StarBloom active />
              </span>
              <h3 class="font-display text-4xl leading-[1.05] font-light sm:text-5xl">{suite.name}</h3>
            </div>
            {suite.description && (
              <p class="prose-measure border-base-300 text-base-content/55 mt-5 border-l pl-5 text-lg italic">
                {suite.description}
              </p>
            )}

            <div id="al-suite" class="al-suite text-base-content mt-16 flex flex-col gap-16">
              {suite.items.map((f, i) => (
                <article
                  class:list={['al-stanza flex gap-4 sm:gap-6', f.kind === 'writing' && 'al-writing']}
                  style={`--indent:${SUITE_INDENTS[i % SUITE_INDENTS.length]}`}
                >
                  {/* The span stays even with the glyph hidden: it is the gutter
                      that holds the text column off the margin AND the anchor the
                      star figure measures its joints from. Only the glyph goes. */}
                  <span
                    class="al-mark text-base-content/35 mt-1.5 w-5 shrink-0 text-center font-serif text-base select-none"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  >
                    <span class="al-glyph">{glyph[f.kind]}</span>
                  </span>
                  <div class="min-w-0 grow">
                    <SuiteStanza item={f} />
                    {f.kind === 'writing' && (
                      <a
                        href={`/blog/${f.item.slug}`}
                        data-read={f.item.slug}
                        class="al-cta al-scta"
                        tabindex="-1"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                      >
                        <span class="al-cta__word">Read</span>
                        <span class="al-cta__arrow">→</span>
                      </a>
                    )}
                  </div>
                </article>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
            {SUITE_PICKS.map((p) => (
              <p class="text-base-content/50 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed">
                <strong class="text-base-content/70">{p.label}</strong> — {p.blurb}
              </p>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {/* ═══ 3 · the four, side by side ═══════════════════════════════════════ */}
    {
      suite && sampleWriting && (
        <section class="mb-28">
          <Label as="h2" variant="voice" class="mb-2">
            3 · The four, on one pair
          </Label>
          <p class="prose-measure text-base-content/60 mb-8 text-sm">
            The same essay four ways, each with the same quote beneath it — because the question is never “can I see the
            cue”, it is “can I tell these two apart”. These four keep their treatment no matter what the bar says, so
            they can be compared directly. Hover still applies here if it is on.
          </p>

          <div class:list={['divide-base-300 border-base-300 divide-y rounded-lg border', `cn-${suite.color}`]}>
            {SUITE_PICKS.map((p) => (
              <div class="px-5 py-8 sm:px-8" data-suite={p.key} data-fixed>
                <div class="text-base-content/45 mb-5 font-sans text-xs">{p.label}</div>
                <div class="al-suite flex flex-col gap-10">
                  <article class="al-stanza al-writing flex gap-4 sm:gap-6">
                    <span
                      class="al-mark text-base-content/35 mt-1.5 w-5 shrink-0 text-center font-serif text-base select-none"
                      aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                      <span class="al-glyph">{glyph.writing}</span>
                    </span>
                    <div class="min-w-0 grow">
                      <SuiteStanza item={sampleWriting} />
                      {sampleWriting.kind === 'writing' && (
                        <a
                          href={`/blog/${sampleWriting.item.slug}`}
                          data-read={sampleWriting.item.slug}
                          class="al-cta al-scta"
                          tabindex="-1"
                          aria-hidden="true"
                        >
                          <span class="al-cta__word">Read</span>
                          <span class="al-cta__arrow">→</span>
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </article>
                  {sampleQuote && (
                    <article class="al-stanza flex gap-4 sm:gap-6">
                      <span
                        class="al-mark text-base-content/35 mt-1.5 w-5 shrink-0 text-center font-serif text-base select-none"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                      >
                        <span class="al-glyph">{glyph.quote}</span>
                      </span>
                      <div class="min-w-0 grow">
                        <SuiteStanza item={sampleQuote} />
                      </div>
                    </article>
                  )}
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {/* ═══ 4 · what each one costs ══════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    <section class="mb-24">
      <Label as="h2" variant="voice" class="mb-4">4 · What each one costs</Label>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full font-sans text-sm">
          <thead class="text-base-content/45 text-left text-xs">
            <tr class="border-base-300 border-b">
              <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-normal">candidate</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-normal">for</th>
              <th class="py-2 font-normal">against</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-base-300/60 text-base-content/70 divide-y align-top">
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">sky · lamplight</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >Already the house language, and unambiguous here because every row is a link. Answers touch on its own
                terms rather than faking a pointer.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >Motion on the front door. And it only speaks while you scroll — a visitor who lands and stops sees
                nothing.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">sky · warm at rest</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >Works before any interaction, including on a screenshot. The oldest signal there is: links are a
                different colour.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3">Spends the page’s stillness. Eleven coloured names is a menu, not a sky.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">sky · a mark</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Unmistakable, and cheap — one glyph, no colour spent.</td>
              <td class="py-3"
                >Eleven arrows down a page whose whole argument is restraint. Closest thing here to chrome.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">sky · E, the composite</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >Says it three ways at once, which is what discoverability actually needs — a stranger only has to catch
                one of them. And they are one gesture, not three: a single colour at two intensities, with the mark and
                the word riding the same focus.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >The most expensive answer on the page, and the only one with moving parts. Motion on every row is a
                standing debt: it has to survive being seen a hundredth time by someone who lives here.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">sky · the intro line</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >One key for the whole page instead of a cue per row, and it gives the room the visible heading it has
                never had.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >It is instruction. If the Sky is not allowed to teach, this is out and the burden falls entirely on
                ornament.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-base-300 border-t-2">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">suite · a word</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >The only candidate that says WHETHER rather than hinting. In the attribution’s own register, so it
                reads as typeset, not as UI.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3">It is a read-more by another name, and it repeats down the column once per essay.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">suite · the margin mark</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >No chrome in the text column at all, and it reuses a glyph already on screen doing a related job.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >Asks the visitor to decode a symbol. Useless to anyone scanning the text rather than the margin — and
                it lands on top of a star.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">suite · warm at rest</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >Silent and instant — an essay is simply a different ink from a quote, no glyph, no word.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >Competes with the lamplight for the same channel. Once the whole column warms as you scroll, a resting
                warmth is hard to read as meaning.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">suite · E, the composite</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >Turns the lamplight from the problem into the carrier: it still passes over everything, but only the
                openable stanzas have a word for it to warm, so the passing light finally distinguishes instead of
                flattening. One philosophy across both surfaces, learned once.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >A word under every essay in a suite composed to be read straight through — the repetition a quote never
                pays. And it leans on a distinction a reader must notice ACROSS stanzas to decode.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">suite · dropping the glyphs</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >Answers a question nobody asked — a reader can see that a quote is a quote — and hands the margin back
                to the star, which says the thing worth saying in the site's own vocabulary.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >The star was sized to sit BEHIND a glyph that was doing duty as its opaque body. Removing it promotes
                the halo to the whole mark, so the figure may need resizing rather than merely uncovering.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">both · the bloom on focus</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >The overview's most expressive gesture has been hover-only since it shipped, which means the Sky's real
                audience has never once seen it. Moving it onto focus is the cheapest win here — nothing new is drawn,
                an existing thing is merely made reachable.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3"
                >It now fires from SCROLLING, not from intent. A gesture built to reward attention starts happening to
                people who are merely passing.</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap">both · press feedback</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4"
                >Not a candidate — table stakes. Today a tap confirms nothing until the page changes, which on a slow
                connection reads as “broken”.</td
              >
              <td class="py-3">None. Ship it whichever cue wins.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="prose-measure text-base-content/50 mt-6 text-sm">
        ⚠ One bug found on the way in, independent of every choice above: app.css’s hover rules are not wrapped in
        <code>@media (hover: hover)</code> (the file uses that guard in two other places). On touch a tap can leave a stanza
        stuck in its hover colour after you move on — which makes the mixed-list problem worse, since an inert quote beside
        a permanently-warm essay reads as noise rather than as meaning.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="border-base-300 text-base-content/30 border-t pt-8 font-sans text-xs">
      Local prototype — dev only, never deployed. §2 and §3 render the shipped <code>SuiteStanza</code> and the real
      <code>Reader</code>; §1 is rebuilt with lab-scoped classes so its hover rules can be gated.
    </footer>
  </main>

  {/* the real Reader, so a tap in §2/§3 actually arrives somewhere */}
  <Reader />
  {
    writings.map((w) => (
      <template data-reader-content={w.slug}>
        <PostArticle item={w} linkTitle />
      </template>
    ))
  }
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* Lab-scoped by construction: every `.al-` name exists nowhere else and dies
     with this page. The one thing that must never leak is a hover rule — they
     are all written inside `html:not(.al-touch)` so the bar can take them
     away wholesale. app.css belongs to the shipped Sky. */

  /* ── §1 the sky ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .al-star {
    color: var(--cn, var(--color-primary));
    transition: opacity 0.4s ease;
  }
  .al-star .star-mark {
    filter: drop-shadow(
      0 0 calc(7px * var(--glow, 0.6))
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--cn, var(--color-primary)) calc(70% * var(--glow, 0.6)), transparent)
    );
    transition: filter 0.4s ease;
  }
  .al-name {
    transition: color 0.3s ease;
  }
  /* the shipped affordance, and the entire one — gated */
  html:not(.al-touch) .al-row:hover .al-name {
    color: var(--cn, var(--color-primary));
  }
  html:not(.al-touch) .al-row:hover .al-star .star-mark {
    filter: drop-shadow(
      0 0 calc(12px * var(--glow, 0.6)) color-mix(in oklab, var(--cn, var(--color-primary)) 85%, transparent)
    );
  }

  /* B · lamplight — `.is-near` is written by scroll position for every row
     regardless of treatment; only this treatment reacts to it. */
  [data-sky='lamp'] .al-row.is-near .al-name {
    color: var(--cn, var(--color-primary));
  }
  [data-sky='lamp'] .al-row.is-near .al-star .star-mark {
    filter: drop-shadow(
      0 0 calc(12px * var(--glow, 0.6)) color-mix(in oklab, var(--cn, var(--color-primary)) 85%, transparent)
    );
  }

  /* C · warm at rest */
  [data-sky='rest'] .al-name {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--cn, var(--color-primary)) 40%, var(--color-base-content));
  }

  /* D · a mark — sized in `em` so it holds across five type tiers, and kept
     off the baseline gap so it never reflows the name it follows. */
  [data-sky='mark'] .al-name::after {
    content: '→';
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 0.4em;
    font-size: 0.5em;
    vertical-align: 0.15em;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 35%, transparent);
    transition: color 0.3s ease;
  }
  html:not(.al-touch) [data-sky='mark'] .al-row:hover .al-name::after {
    color: var(--cn, var(--color-primary));
  }

  /* ── E · the composite ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     B + C + D read as ONE system rather than three signals, by putting them all
     on a single axis: the constellation's own colour, present at rest and
     brought to full strength on the focused row. Two intensities of one thing,
     not three things.

     Everything here keys on `.is-focus`, which JS writes from three inputs —
     nearest-to-the-reading-line (touch), pointer (mouse), keyboard focus — so
     "focused" means the same thing on every device and there is exactly one
     selector to reason about. `.is-near` stays untouched and keeps driving B
     alone, so the composite can still be compared against the pure version. */
  [data-sky='all'] .al-name {
    /* C's resting trace — quieter than C proper (34% vs 40%), because here it
       is the FLOOR of a range rather than the whole statement. */
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--cn, var(--color-primary)) 34%, var(--color-base-content));
  }
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .al-name {
    color: var(--cn, var(--color-primary));
  }
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .al-star .star-mark {
    filter: drop-shadow(
      0 0 calc(12px * var(--glow, 0.6)) color-mix(in oklab, var(--cn, var(--color-primary)) 85%, transparent)
    );
  }

  /* THE BLOOM, ON FOCUS. In production this is a hover flourish and nothing
     else, so a thumb has never once seen it — the single most expressive thing
     the overview does was reachable only by the input the Sky's real audience
     doesn't have. Same values as app.css's `.group:hover` rules, moved onto the
     state that touch can actually reach. The mark parting into several IS the
     message ("a constellation is drawn BETWEEN stars"), and it arrives on the
     same focus as the colour and the word — one thing opening, not three.

     The transitions are StarBloom's own (0.42/0.46s, its cubic-bezier), which
     run a touch slower than the colour's 0.45s ease and land last. That is the
     right order: the mark commits after the row has already said it is warm. */
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .star-bloom__core {
    scale: 0.72;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .star-bloom__sat {
    opacity: 0.8;
    translate: var(--dx) var(--dy);
  }
  /* Hover keeps blooming under EVERY treatment, exactly as production does —
     otherwise A–D would be quietly worse than the live site and the comparison
     would flatter E. Gated, like every other hover rule on this page. */
  html:not(.al-touch) .al-row:hover .star-bloom__core {
    scale: 0.72;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }
  html:not(.al-touch) .al-row:hover .star-bloom__sat {
    opacity: 0.8;
    translate: var(--dx) var(--dy);
  }

  /* The invitation: a mark that is always there, and a word that is not. */
  .al-cta {
    display: none;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.4em;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    font-variant: all-small-caps;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  [data-sky='all'][data-cta='below'] .al-cta--below,
  [data-sky='all'][data-cta='inline'] .al-cta--inline {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
  /* `below` reserves its line permanently and aligns to the description's
     ml-6, so the invitation lands exactly where your eye finishes reading why
     the constellation exists — and the row is the same height in both states. */
  .al-cta--below {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
    margin-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* WORD AND MARK ARE THE SAME OBJECT, so they rest at the same value and warm
     to the same one. The word was `opacity: 0` at rest for one build and it was
     wrong (Michael, 2026-08-07): in `below` the line is RESERVED whether the
     word shows or not, so hiding it bought nothing and spent a visible gap — a
     lone arrow floating beside a hole where a word evidently belongs. The
     reveal was solving a problem the reserved line had already paid for.

     Stated once for both, then the arrow adds its glow on top. The word keeps
     its 80ms delay so the mark still warms a beat first — one gesture arriving,
     not two things switching on. */
  .al-cta__word,
  .al-cta__arrow {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 30%, transparent);
    transition:
      color 0.45s ease,
      filter 0.45s ease;
  }
  .al-cta__word {
    transition-delay: 0.08s;
  }
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .al-cta__word,
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .al-cta__arrow {
    color: var(--cn, var(--color-primary));
  }
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .al-cta__arrow {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 7px color-mix(in oklab, var(--cn, var(--color-primary)) 65%, transparent));
  }

  /* ⚠ `inline` is the ONE place the word still has to hide, and it is not an
     inconsistency — it is the placement's entire reason for existing. `inline`
     sits in the name's own flex row, so any width it holds at rest is width
     stolen from the name, which is what pushes a long one onto an extra line on
     a phone. It therefore unfurls from zero. `below` has its own line and no
     such debt, so it simply shows the word. Same treatment, different rent.

     ⚠ Keyed on `.al-cta--inline`, NOT on `[data-cta='inline']`. `cta:` is a
     SKY question, but that attribute sits on <main> and would reach straight
     down into §2 and collapse the suite's "Read" to zero width along with it —
     a control silently reaching into a surface it was never asked about. */
  .al-cta--inline .al-cta__word {
    display: inline-block;
    max-width: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    transition:
      color 0.45s ease,
      max-width 0.5s ease,
      opacity 0.5s ease;
    transition-delay: 0.08s;
  }
  [data-sky='all'] .al-row.is-focus .al-cta--inline .al-cta__word {
    max-width: 6em;
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* The beckon — a slow double-nudge, the shape of a "come here" gesture, with
     most of the cycle spent perfectly still. A steady pulse reads as machinery
     (the same reason the overview's stars each breathe on their own phase);
     this reads as something waiting for you.

     Transform only. The glow is a static focus state above rather than part of
     the loop, because animating a filter re-rasterises the element every frame
     and this can be running on eleven rows at once under `beckon: always`. */
  @keyframes al-beckon {
    0%,
    58% {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
    68% {
      transform: translateX(0.3em);
    }
    78% {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
    86% {
      transform: translateX(0.16em);
    }
    94%,
    100% {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
  }
  [data-sky='all'][data-beckon='focus'] .al-row.is-focus .al-cta__arrow,
  [data-sky='all'][data-beckon='always'] .al-cta__arrow {
    animation: al-beckon 3.4s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  /* The global reduced-motion rule only clamps DURATION, which would leave an
     infinite animation flickering — kill it by NAME, the same fix app.css uses
     for `.sky-star`. Colour still fades (a tint is not motion), so the whole
     signal survives; only `inline`'s width unfurl snaps instead of sliding. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .al-cta__arrow {
      animation-name: none;
    }
    .al-cta__word {
      transition-property: color, opacity;
    }
  }

  [data-intro='off'] .al-intro {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── §2/§3 the suite ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .al-suite {
    position: relative;
  }
  .al-stanza {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    #al-suite .al-stanza {
      margin-inline-start: var(--indent, 0%);
    }
  }
  .al-line {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
    overflow: visible;
  }
  .al-figure-star {
    fill: var(--lamp);
    opacity: calc(var(--lit, 0) * 0.7);
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 5px var(--lamp));
  }

  .al-mark,
  .al-stanza blockquote,
  .al-stanza figcaption,
  .al-stanza .suite-attrib,
  .al-stanza .suite-note,
  .al-stanza .sw-title,
  .al-stanza .sw-excerpt {
    transition:
      color 0.5s ease,
      text-shadow 0.5s ease;
  }

  /* THE LAMPLIGHT — copied from app.css because this is the thing on trial.
     Note what it does: a quote and an essay warm identically. On touch it is
     the only feedback the page gives, which is why "everything glows" is what
     a phone learns. */
  .al-stanza.is-lit .al-mark {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 55%, transparent);
  }
  .al-stanza.is-lit blockquote {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 24%, var(--color-base-content));
    text-shadow: 0 0 28px color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 20%, transparent);
  }
  .al-stanza.is-lit figcaption,
  .al-stanza.is-lit .suite-attrib {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 30%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 58%, transparent));
  }
  .al-stanza.is-lit .suite-note {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 18%, var(--color-base-content));
    text-shadow: 0 0 28px color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 14%, transparent);
  }
  .al-stanza.is-lit .sw-title {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 42%, var(--color-base-content));
    text-shadow: 0 0 28px color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 18%, transparent);
  }
  .al-stanza.is-lit .sw-excerpt {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 14%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 76%, transparent));
  }

  /* D · warm at rest — deliberately BELOW `.is-lit` but at lower specificity,
     so the passing lamplight still wins and can warm it further. That overlap
     is the treatment's main risk, and it should be visible, not designed out. */
  [data-suite='rest'] .al-writing .sw-title {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 34%, var(--color-base-content));
  }

  /* B · a word — on the anchor, NOT inside the excerpt: `.sw-excerpt` is a
     line-clamped -webkit-box (an ::after there gets clamped away) and it wears
     the fade mask, which would dim the one thing that has to stay legible. */
  [data-suite='word'] .al-writing a::after {
    content: 'read on';
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    font-variant: all-small-caps;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 55%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 55%, transparent));
    transition: color 0.3s ease;
  }
  html:not(.al-touch) [data-suite='word'] .al-writing:hover a::after {
    color: var(--lamp);
  }

  /* C · the margin mark — a ring around the glyph. The 5 (w-5) column is only
     1.25rem wide and the glyph fills most of it, so the ring has to come from
     padding on a shrunk box rather than a border on the box as it stands. */
  [data-suite='mark'] .al-writing .al-mark {
    box-sizing: content-box;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 45%, transparent);
    border-radius: 999px;
    line-height: 1.15rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 70%, transparent);
    transition:
      color 0.5s ease,
      border-color 0.5s ease;
  }

  /* the shipped hover, gated */
  html:not(.al-touch) .al-writing:hover .sw-title {
    color: var(--lamp);
  }
  html:not(.al-touch) .al-writing:hover .sw-excerpt {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 42%, var(--color-base-content));
  }
  html:not(.al-touch) .al-writing:hover .al-mark {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 75%, transparent);
  }

  /* ── THE TYPE GLYPHS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     ” ♪ ▤ in the margin, off by default (Michael, 2026-08-07): "it's hard for
     the user to tell what those are even for… it's really just a distraction."
     He is right that they answer a question nobody asked — a reader can see
     that a quote is a quote — while spending the one piece of margin the star
     needs. The star says "a fragment lives here", which is the thing worth
     saying, and it says it in the site's own vocabulary.

     ⚠ WATCH THE STARS WHEN YOU TOGGLE THIS. app.css sizes them 22–27px on the
     explicit grounds that they must "clear the fragment's glyph, which paints
     on top of it out to roughly half the 16px em box, or the star is simply
     swallowed" — i.e. the glyph was serving as the star's opaque BODY, and the
     drawn points were only ever its halo. Take the glyph away and the star is
     suddenly the whole mark, at a size chosen for a different job. If the
     figure now reads thin or gappy, that is the sizing to revisit, not this
     decision. */
  [data-glyphs='off'] .al-glyph {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── E · the composite, inside a constellation ─────────────────────────────
     The sky's philosophy, brought in — with the one adjustment the suite forces.
     On the overview E can speak on every row, because every row is a door.
     Here it must speak on SOME stanzas and not others, so the composite is
     applied ONLY to writing.

     That turns out to fix the lamplight rather than fight it. The passing light
     still touches every stanza — it always did, and that was the whole problem,
     because a warming quote and a warming essay said the same thing. But once
     only the openable stanzas carry a word for the light to warm, the light
     stops being the signal and becomes the CARRIER of one. The quote still
     glows; it simply has nothing to say. */
  [data-suite='all'] .al-writing .sw-title {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 34%, var(--color-base-content));
  }
  [data-suite='all'] .al-writing.is-focus .sw-title {
    color: var(--lamp);
    text-shadow: 0 0 28px color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 22%, transparent);
  }
  [data-suite='all'] .al-writing.is-focus .sw-excerpt {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 30%, var(--color-base-content));
  }

  /* The suite's own CTA. Indented to nothing — it sits in the text column, on
     the excerpt's left edge, because unlike the overview there is no margin
     here that isn't already the star's. */
  .al-scta {
    display: none;
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
  }
  [data-suite='all'] .al-scta {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
  [data-suite='all'] .al-writing.is-focus .al-cta__word,
  [data-suite='all'] .al-writing.is-focus .al-cta__arrow {
    color: var(--lamp);
  }
  [data-suite='all'] .al-writing.is-focus .al-cta__arrow {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 7px color-mix(in oklab, var(--lamp) 65%, transparent));
  }
  /* ⚠ DESCENDANT, not compound. `data-beckon` is on <main> and `data-suite` is
     on §2's own container (and on §3's cards) — they are never the same element
     here, unlike on the sky side where both sit on <main>. Written the compound
     way, `[data-suite='all'][data-beckon='focus']`, this matches nothing at all
     and fails silently, which is the worst way for a bench to be wrong. */
  [data-beckon='focus'] [data-suite='all'] .al-writing.is-focus .al-scta .al-cta__arrow,
  [data-beckon='always'] [data-suite='all'] .al-scta .al-cta__arrow {
    animation: al-beckon 3.4s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* ── press: the tap confirmation that exists nowhere today ─────────────── */
  [data-press='on'] .al-row:active,
  [data-press='on'] .al-writing a:active {
    opacity: 0.55;
    transition: opacity 0.06s ease;
  }

  /* which treatment am I looking at — `aria-pressed` told a screen reader and
     nobody else, and two of the four look identical until you press one */
  .lab-pick[aria-pressed='true'] {
    background: var(--color-base-200);
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }
  /* Loud whenever the bench is not showing you your own device — forgetting
     which input model you are judging is the easiest way to draw the wrong
     conclusion here, and `auto` on a laptop looks identical to `pointer`. */
  #t-input[data-forced] {
    background: var(--color-warning);
    color: var(--color-base-100);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { STAR_PATH } from '../lib/star-mark';

  const SVGNS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
  const main = document.querySelector('main') as HTMLElement;

  // ── the reading line ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // The shipped suite DRIFTS this with scroll progress so the light passes
  // through every stanza on a page that is one suite. Here the suite is a
  // section among sections, so progress through the document means nothing —
  // a fixed line is the honest bench equivalent. (Do not copy this back.)
  const LINE = () => window.innerHeight * 0.45;

  // ── §1: nearest row ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Written for every row regardless of treatment; only [data-sky='lamp']
  // reacts, so switching treatments never has to re-run this.
  const rows = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.al-row')];
  let near: HTMLElement | null = null;
  // Whichever of `els` sits closest to the reading line right now, or null if
  // none is on screen — nothing should be lit by a page you cannot see.
  function nearest(els: HTMLElement[]): HTMLElement | null {
    const line = LINE();
    let best: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let bestD = Infinity;
    for (const el of els) {
      const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
      if (r.bottom < 0 || r.top > window.innerHeight) continue;
      const d = Math.abs(r.top + r.height / 2 - line);
      if (d < bestD) {
        bestD = d;
        best = el;
      }
    }
    return best;
  }

  function updateNear() {
    const best = nearest(rows);
    if (best !== near) {
      near?.classList.remove('is-near');
      best?.classList.add('is-near');
      near = best;
    }
    applyFocus();
  }

  // ── `.is-focus`: ONE state, three inputs ─────────────────────────────────
  // Used by BOTH composites — E on the sky and E in the suite — so "focused"
  // means the same thing on a phone, a trackpad and a keyboard, on either
  // surface, and each has a single selector to reason about instead of a
  // :hover rule, a proximity rule and a :focus-visible rule kept in sync by
  // hand forever.
  //
  // ⚠ PRECEDENCE MATTERS, and it is the reason this is JS rather than
  // `:is(.is-near, :hover, :focus-visible)`. With CSS alternation a desktop
  // reader lights TWO things — the one they are scrolling past and the one
  // under the cursor — which makes the signal mean "somewhere around here"
  // instead of "this one". Deliberate beats ambient: pointer, then keyboard,
  // then proximity, and exactly one element is ever lit.
  // ⚠ TOUCH AND POINTER ARE TWO MODELS, NOT ONE MODEL PLUS A FALLBACK.
  // (Michael, 2026-08-07: "it gets a little janky or a little too visually busy
  // … now the mouse hover and the scrolling are competing for attention.")
  //
  // He is right, and the first build had the flaw by construction: proximity
  // ran everywhere and hover ran on top of it, so on a desktop TWO systems were
  // narrating at once — the page lighting rows as you scrolled while the cursor
  // lit a different one. Precedence stopped them contradicting each other; it
  // could not stop them both being in motion.
  //
  // So proximity is not a fallback that hover overrides. It is the ANSWER TO A
  // DIFFERENT QUESTION — "what am I looking at, on a device that cannot point?"
  // — and it should not be asked at all where a pointer exists. Each mode picks
  // exactly one ambient input:
  //
  //     touch    keyboard ?? proximity     (pointer never consulted)
  //     pointer  pointer  ?? keyboard      (proximity never consulted)
  //
  // Keyboard works in both, because a keyboard is orthogonal to both.
  const touchMode = () => document.documentElement.classList.contains('al-touch');
  // Every tracker's re-evaluator, so flipping the mode re-decides all of them in
  // one pass. Without this, switching to touch leaves whatever the cursor was
  // last resting on lit by a pointer the page has just stopped believing in.
  const trackers: (() => void)[] = [];

  function focusTracker(els: HTMLElement[]) {
    let pointerEl: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let keyEl: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let proximate: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let current: HTMLElement | null = null;

    const apply = () => {
      const next = touchMode() ? (keyEl ?? proximate) : (pointerEl ?? keyEl);
      if (next === current) return;
      current?.classList.remove('is-focus');
      next?.classList.add('is-focus');
      current = next;
    };
    trackers.push(apply);

    for (const el of els) {
      el.addEventListener('pointerenter', () => {
        pointerEl = el;
        apply();
      });
      el.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => {
        if (pointerEl === el) pointerEl = null;
        apply();
      });
      // focusin/focusout, NOT focus/blur: on the sky the element IS the anchor,
      // but a stanza's focusable link is a descendant, and focus/blur don't
      // bubble. One pair that works for both beats two that each work for one.
      el.addEventListener('focusin', () => {
        keyEl = el;
        apply();
      });
      el.addEventListener('focusout', () => {
        if (keyEl === el) keyEl = null;
        apply();
      });
    }

    return {
      setProximate(el: HTMLElement | null) {
        proximate = el;
        apply();
      },
    };
  }

  const rowFocus = focusTracker(rows);
  function applyFocus() {
    rowFocus.setProximate(near);
  }

  // ── §2: the lamplight + the figure ───────────────────────────────────────
  const suiteEl = document.getElementById('al-suite');
  const items = suiteEl ? [...suiteEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.al-stanza')] : [];
  // §3's fixed cards get their own tracker, or the composite would be dead in
  // the one place it exists to be compared side by side. ⚠ And it needs its own
  // PROXIMITY source, not just pointer: in touch mode a tracker with no
  // proximate never lights anything, so §3 would go dark in exactly the mode
  // §3 is for. One tracker across all eight specimen stanzas, so scrolling past
  // the card stack lights them one at a time the way a phone would.
  const fixedStanzas = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-fixed] .al-stanza')];
  const stanzaFocus = focusTracker(items);
  const fixedFocus = focusTracker(fixedStanzas);
  let svg: SVGSVGElement | null = null;
  let lit: HTMLElement | null = null;
  let starEls: { el: SVGElement; y: number; shown: number }[] = [];

  // The arcs and their stars, verbatim from ConstellationSuite — the figure is
  // here because treatment C rings the margin glyph and the glyph sits ON a
  // star, and a collision is only findable on a page that draws both.
  function drawFigure() {
    if (!suiteEl) return;
    svg?.remove();
    svg = null;
    starEls = [];
    const box = suiteEl.getBoundingClientRect();
    const pts = items.map((el) => {
      const r = el.querySelector('.al-mark')!.getBoundingClientRect();
      return { x: r.left - box.left + r.width / 2, y: r.top - box.top + r.height / 2 };
    });
    const GAP = 14;
    let d = '';
    for (let i = 0; i < pts.length - 1; i++) {
      const p1 = pts[i];
      const p2 = pts[i + 1];
      const dx = p2.x - p1.x;
      const dy = p2.y - p1.y;
      const len = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
      if (len < GAP * 2.5) continue;
      const ux = dx / len;
      const uy = dy / len;
      const a = { x: p1.x + ux * GAP, y: p1.y + uy * GAP };
      const b = { x: p2.x - ux * GAP, y: p2.y - uy * GAP };
      const bow = Math.min(30, len * 0.16) * (i % 2 ? -1 : 1);
      const c = { x: (a.x + b.x) / 2 + -uy * bow, y: (a.y + b.y) / 2 + ux * bow };
      d += `M ${a.x} ${a.y} Q ${c.x} ${c.y} ${b.x} ${b.y} `;
    }
    if (!d) return;
    svg = document.createElementNS(SVGNS, 'svg');
    svg.setAttribute('class', 'al-line');
    svg.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    const path = document.createElementNS(SVGNS, 'path');
    path.setAttribute('d', d);
    path.setAttribute('fill', 'none');
    path.setAttribute('stroke', 'var(--lamp)');
    path.setAttribute('stroke-opacity', '0.22');
    path.setAttribute('stroke-width', '1');
    path.setAttribute('stroke-linecap', 'round');
    svg.appendChild(path);
    pts.forEach((p, i) => {
      const size = 22 + (i % 3) * 2.5;
      const star = document.createElementNS(SVGNS, 'path');
      star.setAttribute('class', 'al-figure-star');
      star.setAttribute('d', STAR_PATH);
      star.setAttribute(
        'transform',
        `translate(${(p.x - size / 2).toFixed(1)} ${(p.y - size / 2).toFixed(1)}) scale(${(size / 24).toFixed(4)})`,
      );
      svg!.appendChild(star);
      starEls.push({ el: star, y: p.y, shown: -1 });
    });
    suiteEl.prepend(svg);
  }

  function updateGlow() {
    const best = nearest(items);
    if (best !== lit) {
      lit?.classList.remove('is-lit');
      best?.classList.add('is-lit');
      lit = best;
    }
    // ⚠ The lamplight itself is NOT input-conditional and stays on every device.
    // It is atmosphere — the light passing over a suite as you read it — and it
    // shipped long before this question was asked. What became touch-only is the
    // AFFORDANCE layer that rides it: on a desktop the stanzas still warm as you
    // scroll, but the "Read →" only answers the cursor. Nothing competes,
    // because only one of the two was ever claiming to be a control.
    stanzaFocus.setProximate(lit);
    fixedFocus.setProximate(nearest(fixedStanzas));
  }

  const FALLOFF = 0.62;
  function updateStars() {
    if (!suiteEl || !starEls.length) return;
    const top = suiteEl.getBoundingClientRect().top;
    const line = LINE();
    const reach = window.innerHeight * FALLOFF;
    for (const s of starEls) {
      const dist = Math.abs(top + s.y - line);
      const k = Math.max(0, 1 - dist / reach);
      const q = Math.round(k * k * (3 - 2 * k) * 50) / 50;
      if (q !== s.shown) {
        s.shown = q;
        s.el.style.setProperty('--lit', String(q));
      }
    }
  }

  let raf = 0;
  function onScroll() {
    cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
    raf = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      updateNear();
      updateGlow();
      updateStars();
    });
  }
  addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
  addEventListener('resize', onScroll);
  new ResizeObserver(() => {
    drawFigure();
    updateStars();
  }).observe(document.body);
  (document as any).fonts?.ready?.then(() => {
    drawFigure();
    updateStars();
  });
  drawFigure();
  onScroll();

  // ── the bar ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // §3's four cards each keep their own treatment forever (data-fixed) — they
  // are the side-by-side, and there is nothing to compare if the bar moves them.
  function setSky(key: string) {
    main.dataset.sky = key;
    for (const b of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-sky-pick]')) {
      b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.skyPick === key));
    }
  }
  // §2's container, never <main> — see the note above it in the markup.
  const suiteScope = document.getElementById('al-suite-scope');
  function setSuite(key: string) {
    if (suiteScope) suiteScope.dataset.suite = key;
    for (const b of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-suite-pick]')) {
      b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.suitePick === key));
    }
  }
  for (const b of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-sky-pick]')) {
    b.addEventListener('click', () => setSky(b.dataset.skyPick!));
  }
  for (const b of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-suite-pick]')) {
    b.addEventListener('click', () => setSuite(b.dataset.suitePick!));
  }
  // E is the default: it is the current front-runner, so the page should open
  // on the thing being decided rather than on the thing being replaced.
  setSky('all');
  setSuite('none');

  // ── input mode ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // `(hover: hover)` is the right question, not `(pointer: coarse)` or a UA
  // sniff: it asks whether the PRIMARY input can hover at all, which is exactly
  // the capability the whole decision turns on. A touchscreen laptop answers
  // yes and gets the pointer model, which is correct — it has a cursor.
  //
  // ⚠ LIVE, not read once. An iPad becomes a hover device the moment a Magic
  // Keyboard is attached and stops being one when it is pulled off, and the
  // same page is still on screen. Reading this at startup would leave that
  // device running the wrong model until a reload it has no reason to perform.
  const canHover = matchMedia('(hover: hover)');

  // auto → follow the device; touch/pointer → force it, for judging the other
  // half from whichever machine you happen to be sitting at.
  const MODES = ['auto', 'touch', 'pointer'] as const;
  let modeChoice: (typeof MODES)[number] = 'auto';
  const inputBtn = document.getElementById('t-input')!;

  function applyInput() {
    const touch = modeChoice === 'auto' ? !canHover.matches : modeChoice === 'touch';
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle('al-touch', touch);
    inputBtn.textContent =
      modeChoice === 'auto' ? `input: auto · ${touch ? 'touch' : 'pointer'}` : `input: ${modeChoice}`;
    inputBtn.classList.toggle('btn-ghost', modeChoice === 'auto');
    if (modeChoice === 'auto') inputBtn.removeAttribute('data-forced');
    else inputBtn.setAttribute('data-forced', '');
    // Re-decide every tracker: the inputs they hold are unchanged, but which of
    // them COUNTS just did.
    for (const t of trackers) t();
  }

  inputBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    modeChoice = MODES[(MODES.indexOf(modeChoice) + 1) % MODES.length];
    applyInput();
  });
  canHover.addEventListener('change', applyInput);
  applyInput();

  // E's two sub-questions. Both cycle rather than toggle — three states each,
  // and a tri-state checkbox is worse than a button that says what it is.
  function cycle(id: string, attr: 'beckon' | 'cta' | 'glyphs', values: string[]) {
    const btn = document.getElementById(id)!;
    const paint = () => (btn.textContent = `${attr}: ${main.dataset[attr]}`);
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const i = values.indexOf(main.dataset[attr] ?? values[0]);
      main.dataset[attr] = values[(i + 1) % values.length];
      paint();
    });
    paint();
  }
  cycle('t-beckon', 'beckon', ['focus', 'always', 'off']);
  cycle('t-cta', 'cta', ['below', 'inline', 'off']);
  // Not a treatment — a decision already taken, kept switchable only so the
  // star figure can be checked against the glyph it used to hide behind.
  cycle('t-glyphs', 'glyphs', ['off', 'on']);

  function toggle(id: string, attr: 'intro' | 'press', label: string) {
    const btn = document.getElementById(id)!;
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const on = main.dataset[attr] !== 'on';
      main.dataset[attr] = on ? 'on' : 'off';
      btn.textContent = `${label}: ${on ? 'on' : 'off'}`;
      btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
    });
  }
  toggle('t-intro', 'intro', 'intro');
  toggle('t-press', 'press', 'press');

  // ⚠ `dusk` (dark, default) and `paper` (light). An earlier lab button wrote
  // `day`, which is not a theme — daisyUI fell back silently and the toggle
  // appeared to work while comparing dusk against dusk.
  const themeBtn = document.getElementById('t-theme')!;
  const syncTheme = () => {
    themeBtn.textContent = document.documentElement.dataset.theme === 'paper' ? 'dusk' : 'paper';
  };
  themeBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    root.dataset.theme = root.dataset.theme === 'paper' ? 'dusk' : 'paper';
    syncTheme();
  });
  syncTheme();
</script>
